      "text": "To solve for \\( x \\) in the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we first need to simplify both logarithmic expressions.\n\nFirst, let's simplify \\(\\log_{27} 3\\). We know that \\(27 = 3^3\\), so we can rewrite the logarithm as:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3\n\\]\nUsing the change of base formula for logarithms, \\(\\log_{a^b} c = \\frac{1}{b} \\log_a c\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_{3^3} 3 = \\frac{1}{3} \\log_3 3\n\\]\nSince \\(\\log_3 3 = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nSo the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis means that \\(x\\) is the base such that:\n\\[\nx^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 4\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we raise both sides of the equation to the power of 3:\n\\[\n( x^{\\frac{1}{3}} )^3 = 4^3\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
