      "text": "Since $\\log_x 4=\\log_{27} 3$, we have $\\log_x 4=\\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}=\\frac{\\log 3}{3\\log 3}=\\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\log_x 4 = \\log_x 2^2 = 2\\log_x 2 = \\frac{1}{3}$, which gives $\\log_x 2 = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}$.\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\) given that \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing the given logarithms in terms of base 10 or natural logarithms. The given equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\n\\]\n\nFirst, we use the change of base formula for logarithms. For the right side, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we can simplify \\(\\log 27\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3 \\log 3\n\\]\n\nThus, we can rewrite the right side of the equation as:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we convert the logarithmic equation into an exponential equation. The equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\\) means that:\n\n\\[\nx^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 4\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we raise both sides of the equation to the power of 3:\n\n\\[\n(x^{\\frac{1}{3}})^3 = 4^3\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( x \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
