      "text": "Given that $\\log_x 4=\\log_{27} 3$, we want to find the value of $x$.\n\nTo do this, we need to express both logarithms in terms of the same base. We will use the change of base formula, which states that $\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}$ for any positive $a$, $b$, and base $c$.\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we can write:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log_{27} 4}{\\log_{27} x}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} 27} = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} (3^3)} = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{3 \\log_{10} 3} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nWe are given that $\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3$, so we can substitute the expression for $\\log_{27} 3$:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_{27} 4}{\\log_{27} x} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find $\\log_{27} 4$. Using the change of base formula again, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 4 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 4}{\\log_{10} 27} = \\frac{\\log_{10} (2^2)}{\\log_{10} (3^3)} = \\frac{2 \\log_{10} 2}{3 \\log_{10} 3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into our previous equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{2 \\log_{10} 2}{3 \\log_{10} 3}}{\\log_{27} x} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log_{10} 2}{3 \\log_{10} 3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\log_{27} x} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log_{10} 2}{3 \\log_{10} 3 \\log_{27} x} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by $3 \\log_{27} x$ to eliminate the denominator, we get:\n\\[\n2 \\log_{10} 2 = \\log_{10} 3 \\log_{27} x.\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by $\\log_{10} 2$, we get:\n\\[\n2 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3 \\log_{27} x}{\\log_{10} 2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for $\\log_{27} x$, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} x = \\frac{2 \\log_{10} 2}{\\log_{10} 3}.\n\\]\n\nWe know that $\\log_{10} 3 = \\frac{1}{\\log_{10} 27}$, so $\\frac{\\log_{10} 2}{\\log_{10} 3} = \\log_{27} 2$.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} x = 2 \\log_{10} 2 = \\frac{2}{\\log_{10} 27}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that $\\log_b a = \\frac{1}{\\log_a b}$, we can rewrite this as:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} x = \\frac{2}{1/\\log_{27} 27} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2.\n\\]\n\nThis means that $x$ is the number such that $27^x = 2^2 = 4$. Therefore, $x = 2$.\n\nSo, the value of $x$ is $\\boxed{2}$.",
